  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cole held Tampa Bay to two runs over 5.2 innings, and Rice delivered twice. But the real story was the game ended after the third.",
    "what_this_means": "Ben Rice's two-homer night gave the lineup breathing room early, and it mattered because the Yankees broke Tampa Bay's back in one inning. They scored five in the third and never looked back. Cole was sharp enough to coast. The bullpen cleaned up. This wasn't a test of character or a comeback. It was control from the jump. Nine strikeouts on the offensive side and the blowout shape meant the game's true tension existed only in the early frames. Once that five-run inning landed, the outcome was settled. Rice didn't have to carry late. The lineup didn't have to find something special. They got ahead early enough that execution in the middle innings didn't demand anything it couldn't deliver. That's the difference between a fragile win and one that breathes easy.",
    "what_to_watch": "Ian Seymour takes the mound for Tampa Bay tonight with a 4.38 ERA, and Schlittler's 1.00 mark across his last four starts suggests the Yankees could control the early frame again.",
